People with cerebral palsy, people with other kind of developmental issues that have always been challenged, the idea that we would get better at communicating makes sense.

233
00:15:33,420 --> 00:15:38,420
Now, for some groups for whom language has always been challenging.

234
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:47,420
Like people who are hard of hearing or deaf, they develop a language that has to do with symbols and hand movements and gestures.

235
00:15:47,420 --> 00:15:52,420
And so sometimes those are things that we might look at for guidance.

236
00:15:52,420 --> 00:16:06,420
Because what they work on is keeping all their signs and signals within this range, which would be helpful as people are starting to develop dementia and their field of awareness might be getting smaller.

237
00:16:06,420 --> 00:16:07,420
All told.

238
00:16:07,420 --> 00:16:13,420
This is not a dimension anybody would wish on their worst enemy.

239
00:16:13,420 --> 00:16:16,420
And it is a really challenging one.

240
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:24,420
So people living with primary progressive aphasia often also live with anxiety and or depression.

241
00:16:24,420 --> 00:16:31,420
Because it's super hard to exist in the world when you have trouble getting your messages in and out.

242
00:16:31,420 --> 00:16:33,420
And the world gets super complicated.

243
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:34,420
Yeah.

244
00:16:34,420 --> 00:16:35,420
So, Tipa, you mentioned about getting an opportunity.

245
00:16:35,420 --> 00:16:36,420
Yeah.

246
00:16:36,420 --> 00:16:45,420
You mentioned about getting an opportunity to hear from some of the folks, our core team members who have been so gracious as to share their stories with us and their experiences.

247
00:16:45,420 --> 00:16:58,420
So I just dropped a link in the chat to our Courageous Conversation page where we have a lot of those recordings stored and we'll have more that we will add on there that you can go and check out and you can hear some of their experiences.

248
00:16:58,420 --> 00:17:05,420
The things that they have brought for us to be able to be aware of that have been helpful for them and the challenges that they have faced over time.

249
00:17:05,420 --> 00:17:06,420
Yeah.

250
00:17:06,420 --> 00:17:14,420
So if you're out there and you're living with FTD, primary progressive aphasia, and you'd like to do a Courageous Conversation, let us know.

251
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:25,420
Because, I mean, or if you have worked with people who have primary progressive aphasia and they have one of these variations of primary progressive, let us know.

252
00:17:25,420 --> 00:17:32,420
I mean, because we're always wanting to allow that space, hold that space, support that space.

253
00:17:32,420 --> 00:17:34,420
So people can share their stories.

254
00:17:34,420 --> 00:17:35,420
Yeah.

255
00:17:35,420 --> 00:17:39,420
And share their story and share their message.

256
00:17:39,420 --> 00:17:43,420
So till next time, take care with your care.

257
00:17:43,420 --> 00:17:47,420
And if you need any support out there, let us know.

258
00:17:47,420 --> 00:17:48,420
Take care.

259
00:17:48,420 --> 00:17:49,420
See you later.

260
